      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "11"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2011",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues our study of division by 2, introduced in the text *Two sides* (<htcite0>). Therefore, before going further, we recommend reading it and reviewing its prerequisites.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we study multiplication tables, we learn that they show the results of repeated addition (<htcite0>). As a consequence, they do not show the values between the multiplication results.\n\nLet's recall the table of 2, which we saw in the previous text (<htcite1>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72dcaa0f-07e3-41de-a637-c5b2f528dc33",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent2\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this table, the numbers increase by 2 each time, and as a result, the odd numbers — 1, 3, 5, 7 and so on — do not appear.\n\nThis is also related to division, which we are currently studying — a fact that leads us to reflect:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: There's some left over...</b><br /><br />If we have 8 apples and want to share them with one other person, each person will get 4 (8/2 = 4).<br /><br />Now, what if we have 5 apples and want to share them with one other person?<br /><br />How would you do it?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_visual",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Visualizing Odd and Even\n\nLet's once again use a sheet of paper to visualize what we are studying.\n\n#### Practice",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1</b><br /><br />1. Take a sheet of paper and divide it as shown in <b>Table 1</b>, so that it has 2 rectangles.<br />2. Take 5 small objects, each smaller than the rectangles, and place one object in each rectangle at a time, so that both rectangles always hold the same number of objects. In <b>Table 1</b>, the rectangles could represent other cut-out pieces of paper.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Sheet divided into 2</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Representation of a blank sheet divided into two parts, each containing two rectangles.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Since we have 5 objects, this means that one is left over. This remainder is shown in **Table 2**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\" style=\"width: 200px;\"><tr><th>Remaining rectangle</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; width: 100px; text-aligh: center; border: none;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Representation of one remaining rectangle</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Beyond the Whole\n\nWhen we write this practice as a fraction, we get the following result:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Representation of the practice in fraction form.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When the numerator (the top part of the fraction) is greater than the denominator (the bottom part), it means there are more objects (numerator) than parts available to distribute (denominator).\n\nAlthough this concept is slightly different from what we have studied before, it does not change the meaning of the denominator, which still represents the whole.\n\nTherefore, we should interpret that the number of objects we have is greater than the number of parts that make up one whole.\n\nOn the other hand, if there were only 2 objects — one for each rectangle — we would have a distribution that represents exactly the whole, as shown in **Calculation 2**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Representation of the practice in fraction form showing a complete whole.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_pattern",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Building Patterns\n\nWhen there is a remainder in divisions, this does not necessarily mean that there was a calculation error. Remainders are part of the constructive steps.\n\n#### Building the Multiplication Table\n\nMultiplication tables, for example, are built by choosing the numbers we want to arrange and the size of the group, which corresponds to the table.\n\nFor example, let's arrange the numbers from 1 to 20 in the table 3 (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72dcaa0f-07e3-41de-a637-c5b2f528dc33",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">Table of 2</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">2</td><td>3</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">4</td></tr> <tr><td>5</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">6</td><td>7</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">8</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">10</td><td>11</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">12</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">14</td><td>15</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">16</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">18</td><td>19</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\">20</td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 3</b>: Table of 2</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To understand the remainders of divisions, let's also look at the following table:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">Table with division remainders by 2</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">1</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">1</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-aligh: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 4</b>: Table with division remainders.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "**Table 3** contains the table of 2, with its values highlighted in blue. Likewise, Table 4 also highlights the value 0 in blue. By comparing both tables, we can conclude:\n- Whenever a number is a multiple of 2 — that is, it appears in the table of 2 — the remainder of the division is zero (<htcite0>).\n- As we move away from a multiple, the remainder increases until it reaches the number immediately before the divisor of the table. In the case of the table of 2, the maximum remainder is 1. When we reach the next multiple, the remainder returns to 0.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7499239c-c7fc-474e-ad20-1745e19a06d2",
                     "text": "Potgieter, P., & Blignaut, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 1-2",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Even and Odd\n\nBecause the remainder of the division by 2 can only be 0 or 1, every number falls into one of two groups:\n\n- **Even numbers**: those whose division by 2 leaves no remainder — 2, 4, 6, 8, 10... They can be shared between two equal sides without anything left over (<htcite0>).\n- **Odd numbers**: those whose division by 2 leaves a remainder of 1 — 1, 3, 5, 7, 9... When they are shared between two equal sides, one object is always left over, just like the rectangle in **Table 2** (<htcite1>).\n\nFor this reason, whenever we know that a number is even, we know it splits into two equal sides; whenever it is odd, we know something will always be left over.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "130b6adb-161d-4fec-98e4-eaa384122753",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Even Number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "e2f5ad28-16f1-4167-8326-ac0a93f74330",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Odd Number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: And the next one?</b><br /><br />If an even number is always followed by an odd number, and an odd number is always followed by an even number, what happens when we add 1 to any number?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Whenever we add 1 to an even number, the remainder of its division by 2 changes from 0 to 1, and the result is odd. Whenever we subtract 1 from an odd number, the remainder returns to 0, and the result is even. Because of this, even and odd numbers always alternate: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6...",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Fingers and Sides\n\nWe can see this alternation even in our own body. As we saw in the previous text (<htcite0>), we have 10 fingers, with 5 on each hand. Ten is even: it divides into two equal sides of 5. Five, however, is odd: the fingers of one hand cannot be divided into two equal groups, since 2 + 2 + 1 leaves one finger aside.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Hands representation. © History Tracers. <span id=\"htImgCopyright\"></span></td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows a person's two hands raised with all fingers extended, each pointing toward a fruit.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This shows that, even when two sides seem perfectly equal, there are quantities that do not fit into them without leaving something over.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we saw that dividing a number by 2 always leaves one of two results: a remainder of 0 or a remainder of 1. Numbers with remainder 0 are even, and numbers with remainder 1 are odd.\n\nWe also observed that these remainders follow a pattern: they alternate between 0 and 1, just as even and odd numbers alternate along the number line. The remainder is always smaller than the divisor; if it is larger, that indicates that our division is incorrect.\n\nHowever, with the knowledge we have so far, although we can already identify the leftovers, we still don't operate with them.\n\nThis leads us to a new reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: How can we find it?</b><br /><br /><b>Sharing</b> puts objects into two equal sides.<br /><b>The remainder</b> is what does not fit into either side.<br /><b>Even and odd</b> depend only on that remainder.<br /><br />So, do we always need to share a whole number to know whether it is even or odd?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "For now, the remainder of the division by 2 is enough to group every number we know into two groups: even and odd. In the texts that follow, we will see what happens when dividing into only two sides stops being enough.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is 0 an even number?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "0 appears in the table of 2 (0 = 2 × 0), so the remainder of 0 divided by 2 is zero. Therefore, 0 is even."
      },
      {
         "question": "Is there a remainder when an even number is divided by 2?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Even numbers are exactly those whose division by 2 gives a remainder of zero: the sharing between two equal sides is complete, with nothing left over."
      }
   ],
   "game_v2": null,
   "date_time": null
}
